<script>
  // Deliberate anti-pattern under test. The native card has no script handler.
  document.getElementById('rp-mouse-card').addEventListener('click', () => {
    location.hash = 'rp-destination';
  });
</script>

<h2>The mouse handler teaches one gesture</h2>
<p class="dek">Tab reaches the first card. Enter leaves the URL unchanged.</p>

<p>The first card has <code>role="link"</code> and <code>tabindex="0"</code>. It can receive keyboard focus. Its script still listens only for a mouse click. In the local check, Enter does nothing; clicking changes the fragment when scripts are enabled. Disable page scripts, and the click stops navigating too.</p>

<p>This is a small implementation with a specific omission. It does not establish that scripted navigation cannot work. It establishes what this handler actually implements.</p>

<p>The WAI-ARIA link pattern makes the ownership of that extra work clear: a link role supplies semantics, while an author using it must provide the expected link behavior. The guide recommends an HTML anchor with an <code>href</code> when available.<sup><a href="#rp-ref-apg">1</a></sup></p>

<h2>The destination can live in the markup</h2>
<p class="dek">The second title has an address before a handler runs.</p>

<p>In the same local check, Tab reaches the anchor and Enter navigates to its fragment. Middle-click opens that destination in another page. Those checks also pass with page scripts disabled. There is no navigation handler on this card.</p>

<figure class="step">
<pre class="rp-code"><code>&lt;article class="resource-card"&gt;
  &lt;h3&gt;&lt;a href="#practice"&gt;Editing practice A&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;A fictional resource for the example.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;
    &lt;input type="checkbox"&gt; Mark this example
  &lt;/label&gt;
&lt;/article&gt;</code></pre>
<figcaption><b>The separation.</b> A destination and a local mark are different controls, with neither inside the other.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>The HTML Standard defines an anchor with <code>href</code> as a hyperlink. Its content model also rules out interactive descendants, nested anchors, and descendants with a specified <code>tabindex</code>.<sup><a href="#rp-ref-html">2</a></sup> A checkbox or a second action therefore needs its own place beside the link.</p>

<p>That separation helps the example say what it does. A title opens the resource. The adjacent checkbox marks this example in its current local form state. It does not claim to save a favorite across visits.</p>

<h2>A bigger click target is a fair request</h2>
<p class="dek">Keep the useful part of a clickable card without hiding its controls inside a link.</p>

<p>A whole card is easier to hit with a pointer than a small title. That is a reasonable design goal. If the card has one destination and no other interactive content, an anchor can wrap suitable block content; the standard permits that shape.<sup><a href="#rp-ref-html">2</a></sup></p>

<p>If the card also has a marking control or another destination, give those controls separate boundaries. Expanding the main link's padding or restructuring its non-interactive content may provide a larger target. Those variants are design options to test, rather than behaviors measured by this experiment.</p>

<p>A router component that already renders a real anchor is also outside the first example's problem. Inspect the delivered element before prescribing a rewrite. JavaScript can enhance a link without removing its address.</p>

